13
00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.500
And they told me, flip the lenses, and which one is better.

14
00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:01.540
And you did the whole process of getting fitted for a pair of glasses.

15
00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:07.600
And I thought nothing of it until I walked in the day and picked them up.

16
00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:11.800
And I picked them up, and I looked at them, and for the first time, I put glasses

17
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.180
on, and all of a sudden, the world completely changed.

18
00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:19.800
Things were sharper. I could see things that I didn't even know existed from across

19
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:25.620
the room. My entire perception, my entire reality really started to change because

20
00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:29.920
now I could actually see the way I was supposed to see.

21
00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:34.920
When we're talking about worldviews, the most simplistic way to sort of see

22
00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:39.060
it and understand it is simply this, is by looking at the world through a set of

23
00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:43.780
lenses. And how you look through those lenses sort of dictates how you see the

24
00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:47.180
world. World view, how I view the world, right?

25
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:53.500
So, but more than that, worldviews and what you should start

26
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:59.260
to now see through the worldview questions that were introduced, that worldviews are

27
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:04.340
foundational. They are life-shaping questions about reality.

28
00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:09.620
See, it's not just the pair of glasses that I'm looking at through to see the world.

29
00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:15.720
It's literally how I'm interpreting it and how I'm living as a result of that view.

30
00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:21.120
So all those students should see very quickly through some of the activities in the

31
00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:26.560
Bible study that a biblical worldview has a profound impact on how we live.

32
00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.820
For this week, I want you to keep three really important things in mind

33
00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.900
as you work through this lesson.

34
00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:40.800
Now, we've talked about things like what apologetics is and we've talked about what

35
00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:43.060
faith is. We've talked about what truth is.

36
00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:48.460
We've talked about logic. We've laid this foundation so that you can continue to

37
00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:53.260
move through this study, but now we need students to really understand what it means

38
00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:56.720
to see the world through a particular lens.

39
00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:01.920
But we don't just want them to see the world through the Christian lens.

40
00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:07.060
We want them to understand the world through a couple other popular ways of seeing,

41
00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:12.960
naturalism and pantheism. Then we're going to talk about what it looks like from a

42
00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:18.600
monotheistic worldview because the way in which other people see the world is is

43
00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:24.560
going to vastly, widely influence how your students start to see the world.

44
00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.780
So when people who see the world differently are involved in making movies and

45
00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:33.100
writing music and doing art and making TV shows and whatever else it is, and writing

46
00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:34.220
and books and everything else,

47
00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:39.180
that worldview is going to get communicated to your students.

48
00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:41.380
So they need to start to be able to see that.

49
00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:45.880
But before we can start into all of that, there's three really important things I

50
00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:48.920
want you to keep in mind because there's some things that are going to come up here

51
00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:50.720
that are going to be pretty impactful.

52
00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:56.140
Number one, don't assume your students have a biblical worldview.

53
00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:03.060
Now, if your group has what most are going to call themselves Christians, let's say

54
00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:07.840
your group is a typical youth group that has the majority of Christians, you might

55
00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:12.820
automatically just sort of assume that, hey, my students have a Christian worldview

56
00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:16.860
or a biblical worldview. That may not necessarily be the case.

57
00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:22.200
You're going to discover that your students have some inconsistencies and some

58
00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:23.760
contradictions in their worldview.

59
00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:28.640
In fact, worldviews are often, if you can imagine, going to a buffet restaurant.

60
00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:31.520
Worldviews are often like that now.

61
00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.040
I mean, think about all the information that we have.

62
00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:37.980
Think about what's available to our students via the internet.

63
00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:41.860
That as soon as they want to know something, what do we ask?

64
00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:47.740
We ask Google. We ask Siri. We ask the questions over the internet to figure out the

65
00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:51.040
answers to our questions rather than asking, you know, we're going to a library and

66
00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:52.460
looking at a book and considering the source.

67
00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.020
We just ask. And we look at the top five views on Google, and we go with it.

68
00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:01.440
Our students have more information available to them than any other time in history.

69
00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:06.160
So what happens is students have what I call a buffet worldview.

70
00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:10.840
You walk into a restaurant that's got a buffet, you grab a plate and you just

71
00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:14.260
start taking food that looks good and you just start grabbing whatever that you

72
00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:18.840
want. And then when you're done with that, you get rid of that plate, you go back to

73
00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:22.200
the buffet and you grab different stuff that looked good the next time.

74
00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:26.480
Or maybe you go back and grab some seconds of stuff that you really liked, but you

75
00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:31.100
grab it all together and you just eat at a very low discounted price, right?

76
00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:37.360
So you may just automatically think that they have a biblical worldview because

77
00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:43.060
somewhere along that buffet, there are pieces of Christianity, pieces of Scripture

78
00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:48.180
in their worldview. But then as you explore deeper, especially your small group

79
00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:53.440
leaders, as they explore deeper and get more into conversations and build deeper

80
00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:56.900
relationships with those students, they're going to discover that there's some

81
00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:00.060
significant inconsistencies. Now, don't panic.

82
00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:06.100
That's okay. Because as we move through this process, we'll hopefully iron

83
00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:10.320
out those inconsistencies and help them and guide them through a more consistent

84
00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:10.700
worldview.

85
00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:14.960
That's what the actual worldview questions are designed to do, right?

86
00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:21.000
By asking those four questions, it draws out what they think, but it also reveals

87
00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:24.240
the flaws in thinking in a nice, gentle, respectful way.

88
00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:29.620
So make sure, encourage, and hopefully you've created this environment already,

89
00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:32.880
but encourage your students to answer honestly.

90
00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:36.240
We don't want the answers that they think we want to hear.

91
00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:39.800
We want their actual answers, whatever they may be.

92
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.980
So don't freak out if you hear something that just totally you did not expect.

93
00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:46.260
Number two,

94
00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:50.700
worldviews have to be coherent and consistent.

95
00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:55.770
Even though the buffet sounds really good and it works for some, at least seems like

96
00:06:55.770 --> 00:07:00.590
it works for some, it doesn't. And over time that will cause problems.

97
00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:03.610
That's why you go back for seconds and you take different things because it's a

98
00:07:03.610 --> 00:07:06.290
search for trying to figure out the best possible combination.

99
00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:10.270
That doesn't work. So you're going to, you know, throughout this process we're going

100
00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:15.150
to be helping students understand that this is what makes Christianity so

101
00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:20.110
compelling, is that it's entirely coherent and it's entirely consistent.

102
00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:25.450
And finally, don't expect students to change their worldview right away.

103
00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:29.770
Just because you see that they have a different worldview, or they have

104
00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:33.010
inconsistencies in their worldview, or there's some questions in their worldview,

105
00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:38.090
and you present even at the end of this 32 weeks, if you're presenting the Christian

106
00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:42.190
worldview, don't expect them that they are just going to go, oh, I'm going to put on

107
00:07:42.190 --> 00:07:45.210
a whole new pair of glasses, and I'm going to see the world totally different.

108
00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:49.750
It doesn't work that way. In fact, it doesn't work that way at all.

109
00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:55.490
In fact, changing how we see the world is not as easy as just changing a

110
00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:56.630
prescription on a pair of glasses.

111
00:07:57.050 --> 00:08:02.690
It's a huge, huge adjustment. In fact, sometimes so

112
00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:08.590
big that it causes discomfort. It causes anxiety because people to change how

113
00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:13.270
you see the world and is to really change almost your entire reality of how you

114
00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:17.230
understand things and how you answer big questions in life and how you interact with

115
00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:22.310
people. That's a lot of change, so be patient with your students.

116
00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:25.730
Walk alongside them. Help them figure it all out.

117
00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:31.470
All right, so over the last several weeks, we've sort of challenged you with a lot

118
00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:35.510
of adjustments that you kind of need to make within the context of your group.

119
00:08:35.650 --> 00:08:39.270
We've talked about the idea of building relationships being the most important

120
00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:44.090
thing, Not just the relationship between your students and Christ, but your

121
00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:45.730
relationships between you and your students.

122
00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:47.890
That there needs to be that sense of trust built.

123
00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:52.450
We've also talked about the idea of really just fostering a community where

124
00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:54.030
questions and doubt are welcomed.

125
00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:58.790
We've talked about this idea of worldviews and what this looks like and being

126
00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:03.630
patient with your students in determining what their worldview actually is and to

127
00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:09.210
not be surprised if somehow their worldview doesn't quite line up with what

128
00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:12.870
you thought. And we've even talked about sort of how to get rid of that barrier wall

129
00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:17.450
between what you're talking about in terms of objective truth and their view of

130
00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:22.690
truth in a very post-modern, relativistic, post-truth kind of world that they're

131
00:09:22.690 --> 00:09:27.810
consistently exposed to in school and in media and whatnot.

132
00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:33.190
That's a lot. That's a lot to take in over the course of four weeks.

133
00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:38.250
So now would be a really really good time if you haven't done so already to check in

134
00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:42.090
with your small group leaders checking with your other leaders to see how these

135
00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:47.130
things are going to make sure that the community is starting to coalesce together

136
00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:51.570
the students feel like they can ask the questions check in with some of your key

137
00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:56.430
students who have the pulse of the youth group to sort of get a feel for where

